# Eastern Keel | Animation and Interaction | 2026

*Standards verified June 2026. Assumes Tailwind CSS v4 and Next.js 16.2 App Router. Read before any motion, transition, or interaction work.*

## Philosophy

Animation is not decoration. Every motion serves a purpose. It reveals content, confirms an interaction, guides attention, or communicates hierarchy. The feeling is editorial and alive, like turning a page in a well made magazine that somehow responds to you. Motion is subtle but present. Nothing sits dead. The cumulative effect is a site that feels handcrafted without ever feeling busy.

Rules that never break:

- No colored drop shadows, ever.
- No bounce or elastic easing, ever.
- No zoom in entrance that scales above 1.0.
- No simultaneous full page motion. Stagger everything.
- All motion respects `prefers-reduced-motion`, handled granularly, never with a single blanket kill switch.
- Motion enhances content. It never delays access to it.
- CSS first. Reach for JavaScript only when CSS cannot express the effect. Modern CSS (`@starting-style`, scroll driven animations, View Transitions, anchor positioning, `linear()`) now covers nearly everything that used to need a library.
- No custom cursor. The system cursor is the cursor.

---

## Easing tokens

Define once in the Tailwind v4 theme. Never use the linear, ease in, or ease out defaults. Never bounce or elastic.

```css
/* globals.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --ease-out-soft:   cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);    /* most entrances */
  --ease-in-soft:    cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);    /* exits */
  --ease-inout-soft: cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.55, 1);   /* hovers, toggles */
  --ease-editorial:  cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);   /* hero type reveals */
  --ease-hover:      cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94);
}
```

Tailwind v4 generates `ease-out-soft`, `ease-editorial`, and the rest as utilities from these variables. If a genuinely spring like curve is ever needed, use the CSS `linear()` easing function with a generated point list, never a JavaScript spring library. The house style avoids springs. This is an escape hatch, not an invitation.

---

## Duration tokens

```css
@theme {
  --duration-instant:  80ms;   /* toggles, checkbox, switch */
  --duration-fast:     150ms;  /* hover color, border, opacity */
  --duration-base:     300ms;  /* hover transforms, CTA transitions */
  --duration-moderate: 500ms;  /* scroll reveals, card entrances */
  --duration-slow:     700ms;  /* hero entrances, section reveals */
  --duration-xslow:    1000ms; /* display type, full page transitions */
}
```

Never animate a single element longer than 1000ms. A staggered sequence may run longer in total, which is fine.

---

## Page load: the page assembles in layers

Navigation arrives first, then the hero, then content below. Each layer staggers in with a slight upward drift and fade. The page feels composed, not popped in.

```css
nav { animation: nav-in 500ms var(--ease-out-soft) both; }
@keyframes nav-in { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-8px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }

.hero-image { animation: image-in 1000ms var(--ease-out-soft) 200ms both; }
@keyframes image-in { from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(1.04); } to { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); } }

.hero-sub { animation: fade-up 600ms var(--ease-out-soft) 500ms both; }
.hero-cta { animation: fade-up 600ms var(--ease-out-soft) 650ms both; }
@keyframes fade-up { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(16px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
```

### Display headline, word by word

Split the headline into words and clip reveal each, rising from below a masked line. Pure CSS so it runs before JavaScript and never flashes invisible.

```tsx
// components/ui/SplitHeadline.tsx
export function SplitHeadline({ text, className, delay = 0 }: { text: string; className?: string; delay?: number }) {
  const words = text.split(' ')
  return (
    <span className={className} aria-label={text}>
      {words.map((word, i) => (
        <span key={i} className="inline-block overflow-hidden" aria-hidden="true">
          <span className="inline-block animate-word-in"
                style={{ animationDelay: `${delay + i * 60}ms`, animationFillMode: 'both' }}>
            {word}&nbsp;
          </span>
        </span>
      ))}
    </span>
  )
}
```

```css
@keyframes word-in { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(100%); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
.animate-word-in { animation: word-in 700ms var(--ease-editorial) both; }
```

### Rule lines draw themselves

```css
.rule-draw { transform-origin: left center; animation: rule-in 800ms var(--ease-out-soft) 300ms both; }
@keyframes rule-in { from { transform: scaleX(0); opacity: 0; } to { transform: scaleX(1); opacity: 1; } }
```

SSR safety: anything gated on JavaScript state must render visible by default and animate only once the runtime confirms it can. Pure CSS animations are safe because they need no JavaScript.

