## Page transitions: View Transitions API

Between routes, use the View Transitions API. Same document view transitions have been Baseline across Chrome, Safari, and Firefox since October 2025. Cross document transitions work in Chrome and Safari, with Firefox falling back to an instant navigation, which is acceptable.

The page is replaced, not thrown across the screen. Opacity plus a small vertical drift, nothing more.

```css
::view-transition-old(root) { animation: vt-out 200ms var(--ease-in-soft) both; }
::view-transition-new(root) { animation: vt-in 400ms var(--ease-out-soft) both; }
@keyframes vt-out { to { opacity: 0; } }
@keyframes vt-in { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
```

For finer control than a single root crossfade, assign a `view-transition-class` to a group of elements (cards in a grid, gallery images) and animate the class once rather than naming each element. Use `:active-view-transition` to scope styles that should apply only while a transition is running. Give the fixed nav its own `view-transition-name` so it stays put while the content swaps.

```css
:root::view-transition-group(.card) { animation-duration: 300ms; }
```

Reduced motion turns transitions off:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  ::view-transition-old(root), ::view-transition-new(root) { animation: none; }
}
```

In Next.js App Router, use the `next-view-transitions` package for client work. The official `experimental.viewTransition` flag with React's `<ViewTransition>` component is available but still experimental, so reserve it for projects that accept experimental flags.

