## Parallax, used sparingly

One or two moments per page maximum. Never on mobile. Never on text, only images and background elements. Prefer the CSS scroll driven version behind `@supports`, with a JavaScript hook fallback only where the effect must also run in Firefox. Every JavaScript path checks reduced motion and gates on `(hover: hover)` or width before running.

```css
@supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
  .parallax-image {
    animation: parallax-drift linear both;
    animation-timeline: scroll(root);
  }
  @keyframes parallax-drift { from { transform: translateY(0); } to { transform: translateY(120px); } }
}
```

---

## Loading states

No spinners, anywhere. An image uses a subtle shimmer sweep while loading. A navigation uses a thin 1px progress line at the top of the viewport in the accent color, scaling from left to right via `transform`.

```css
.image-skeleton {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.04) 25%, rgba(0,0,0,0.08) 50%, rgba(0,0,0,0.04) 75%);
  background-size: 200% 100%;
  animation: shimmer 1.5s infinite;
}
@keyframes shimmer { from { background-position: 200% 0; } to { background-position: -200% 0; } }
```

The shimmer gradient is a loading affordance, not a decorative gradient, and is the one allowed exception to the no gradient rule.

---

## Long pages: content-visibility

On marketing pages five screens or longer, add `content-visibility: auto` to below the fold sections so the browser skips their render work until they approach the viewport. Always pair with `contain-intrinsic-size` or scrollbars jump and anchor links break. Never apply it to the hero or anything above the fold.

```css
.section-deferred { content-visibility: auto; contain-intrinsic-size: auto 600px; }
```

---

## Focus states

Focus rings are visible and clean, never the default outline removed without a replacement. They animate in. The ring meets 3:1 contrast against its background.

```css
:focus-visible { outline: none; box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 var(--color-accent); animation: focus-ring-in 150ms var(--ease-out-soft) forwards; }
@keyframes focus-ring-in { to { box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--color-accent); } }
```

---

## prefers-reduced-motion: granular, non negotiable

Reduced motion does not mean no animation. It means no motion that can trigger vestibular disorders: large translations, parallax, zoom, spin, and the kinetic weight shift. Opacity fades and color transitions are safe and should survive. The blanket `* { animation-duration: 0.01ms }` reset is a blunt instrument that kills safe fades. The 2026 standard is opt in motion.

**Pattern 1, preferred. Motion is added, never removed.** Write the static state as the default, add motion only inside the no preference query.

```css
.reveal { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .reveal { /* translateY entrances, parallax, scale, kinetic weight live here */ }
}
```

**Pattern 2. Reduce, do not remove.** Swap movement for a fade under `reduce`.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .reveal, .hero-image, .animate-word-in, .display-kinetic {
    animation: fade-only 300ms ease both; transform: none !important;
  }
  @keyframes fade-only { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
}
```

**JavaScript animations** (count up, parallax, scroll linked) each check the preference before running.

```ts
const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches
if (!prefersReduced) { /* run */ }
```

**Safety net, optional, the floor not the strategy.** A global override may ship as a final fallback. Keep 0.01ms rather than 0ms so `animationend` and `transitionend` still fire. If it is present, the granular patterns above must still be implemented. Never ship the net alone.

---

## What to never do

- No colored drop shadows, on anything.
- No bounce or elastic easing.
- No scale above 1.0 on entrance (images may start at 1.04 and settle to 1).
- No rotation animations except sidebar labels, which are already rotated statically.
- No full page slide transitions.
- No loading spinners. Shimmer or the progress line only.
- No hover animation longer than 300ms.
- No element starting at opacity 0 before JavaScript loads. Pure CSS or SSR safe initial states only.
- No animating layout properties (width, height, margin, padding). Transform and opacity only.
- No parallax on mobile.
- No custom cursor. A circle chasing the pointer is decoration.
- No animation library (Motion, GSAP) unless an effect is provably impossible in CSS, and never for entrances, hovers, or scroll reveals.
- No more than three elements entering at once without stagger.
- No kinetic type on more than one element, and never on body copy.
- No blanket reduced motion kill switch as the only handling.

---

## Implementation checklist

- [ ] Easing and duration tokens defined in `@theme`.
- [ ] Page load staggers nav, headline, hero image, and CTA.
- [ ] Rule lines draw via `transform`, not `width`.
- [ ] Toggled content animates via `@starting-style` and `allow-discrete`, not JavaScript classes.
- [ ] Menus and tooltips placed with anchor positioning, opened with the Popover API.
- [ ] All hover images sit in an `overflow: hidden` wrapper.
- [ ] CTA arrows slide on hover.
- [ ] Scroll reveals: Intersection Observer baseline present, scroll driven version behind `@supports`.
- [ ] Stat numbers count up with a reduced motion check.
- [ ] At most one kinetic display headline, reduced motion gated.
- [ ] Page transitions use the View Transitions API, nav excluded by its own name.
- [ ] `content-visibility: auto` with `contain-intrinsic-size` on long below the fold sections.
- [ ] Reduced motion handled granularly, JavaScript paths gated, safety net optional on top.
- [ ] No colored shadows, no bounce easing, no custom cursor anywhere in the file.
